{
  "revised": "I heard, then, that near Naucratis in Egypt there was one of the ancient gods of that region, the one whose sacred bird is called the ibis; and the divinity’s own name was Theuth. It was he who first invented number and calculation, geometry and astronomy, as well as draughts and dice, and in particular letters. At that time Thamus was king over all Egypt, residing in the great city of the upper country that the Greeks call Egyptian Thebes, and the god himself they call Ammon. Theuth went to him, displayed his arts, and declared that they ought to be imparted to the rest of the Egyptians. The king asked what benefit each art possessed, and as Theuth explained, Thamus censured one aspect and praised another, according as he thought Theuth spoke well or ill. Thamus is said to have declared many things to Theuth on both sides regarding each art, which would take a long account to review. But when they came to letters, Theuth said, 'This branch of study, O King, will make the Egyptians wiser and improve their memory; for a remedy for memory and wisdom has been discovered.' But Thamus replied, 'O most ingenious Theuth, one person has the ability to give birth to the arts, but another must judge what measure of harm or benefit they hold for those who are to use them. And now you, being the father of letters, have out of fondness stated the very opposite of what they can do. For this invention will produce forgetfulness in the souls of those who learn it through lack of practice of their memory, since, relying on writing, they will be reminded from the outside by alien marks, not from within by themselves. It is not a remedy for memory that you have discovered, but for reminding. And to your pupils you offer the semblance of wisdom, not truth; for having become widely informed without instruction, they will seem to have wide knowledge, while for the most part being ignorant and difficult to get along with, having become seeming-wise instead of wise.'",
  "accepted": "I accepted points 1, 2, and 3 regarding φάρμακον, μνήμη, and ὑπόμνησις: replacing 'elixir' with 'remedy' removes the anachronistic alchemical connotations and properly preserves the therapeutic ambivalence, while tightening 'lack of practice of memory' and contrasting 'reminding' with recollecting 'by themselves from within' restores Plato's precise distinctions. I accepted points 4 and 5: translating δόξαν as 'semblance', ἀλήθειαν as 'truth', and δοξόσοφοι as 'seeming-wise' preserves the recurring δόξα-word family and prevents over-psychologizing. I also accepted point 6, correcting the apposition so that the Greeks call the king Thamus the god Ammon, and point 7, adopting 'divinity' for δαίμονι, 'in particular letters' for καὶ δὴ καὶ γράμματα, and a closer rendering of τὸ μὲν ἔψεγεν, τὸ δʼ ἐπῄνει. I rejected only the subsidiary suggestion in point 1 to use 'potion' or 'drug', as 'remedy' best sustains the cognitive and educational metaphor throughout both passages in natural modern English."
}
